Yes, featureCounts does work with a BAM file that contains both paired and unpaired reads and it counts each read pair only once when -p option is provided. If -p is not specified, it will count reads instead of fragments, ie. each read pair may be counted twice.

When there are unpaired reads found in the input, featureCounts converts them to a format that is accepted by featureCounts. The output info of featureCounts is a bit unclear regarding this and we will improve it in the next release (in a week or so).

Regarding your question (2), no reads will be double assigned.

For your question (3), the percentage of successfully assigned reads depends on both read mapping results and the annotation you use. Having a bam including mapped reads only does not mean every read in the file should be assigned. Only mapped reads that overlap with features included in your annotation will be assigned.

Best regards,
Wei

